 QUOTE-TO.SCR   A Parser Utility:  ">  ("quote-to")              ( ********************************************************** )  (       FORTH-83 Standard Program, for passing strings       )  (      to words that get a <name> from the input stream.     )  ( ********************************************************** )  See p. 24, _FORTH-83 STANDARD_, for a definition of "<name>".                                                                   Copyright (c) 1990 Robert Berkey                                   Reproductions for non-commercial purposes authorized.           Code herein is in the public domain.                         GEnie E-mail address: R.BERKEY                                  Directory:                                                                    FORTH-83 Standard Program             4 LOAD       Appendix A   Definitions for Testing               26 28 THRU   Appendix B   FORTH-83 Implementation-Basis Notes   29-31                                                                         Notation Notes   *****************************************                                                                    Stack notation:                                                 ( exectn-state:   input-text   \ x0 -- x1   ;r --   ;p ___ )                                               ( "pronunciation" )                                                                     exectn-state:              see Execution-state notation         input-text   \             see Input-text descriptors           Parameter-stack notation:                                          x                       arbitrary bits                          w                       wrap-around math, no fixed range        addr len                a string                                Flag                    0 or -1                                 flag                    0 or non-zero                        ;r                         Return stack                         ;p                         Purpose                             -----   Notation Notes, cont.                                                                                                   Execution-state notation:                                                                                                          compiled behavior is indented three spaces                                                                                      compiling:      action during compilation                          runtime:     action of compiled code                         interpreting:   action during interpretation                                                                                    defining:       like "compiling:"                                  child:       like "runtime:"                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     cont.    -----   Notation Notes, cont.                                                                                                   Input-text descriptors:                                                                                                            Wordname      A sequence of characters without spaces,                           delimited by a space                           Word          A Wordname with a definition                      <name>Word    A Word that gets a <name> from the input                           stream.                                                                                                     Style notes:                                                                                                                       \ ---         Marks the end of a section of code                                                                          \ -----   End of Notation Notes ---                                                                                              ( FORTH-83 Standard Program, Prelude   ********************* )                                                                  FORTH-83  ( Ensure that a FORTH-83 Standard System is present)                                                                  FORTH DEFINITIONS   DECIMAL   ( initialize variables )                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           6 LOAD                                                           FORTH-83 Standard Program Documentation Requirements                                                                          Minimum requirements for:                                       1. Dictionary space in bytes:              52 bytes                  ( a typical indirect threaded     )                             ( system with headers: 1128 bytes )                        2. Data stack space in bytes:              16 bytes                  ( compiling QUOTE-TO.SCR: 8 bytes )                             ( "> when compiling:      4 bytes )                        3. Return stack space in bytes:             6 bytes             4. Mass storage block ranges:              none                      ( QUOTE-TO.SCR: 32 screens )                               5. Operators's terminal facilities:        none                                                                                \ -----   End of FORTH-83 Standard Program, Prelude ---                                                                          ( System Level Definitions   ******************************* )                                                                  ( Interpreter utilities )                                                                                                       DECIMAL   ( In case the system has reset BASE )                                                                                 32 CONSTANT 20;   ( ;p Hex literal for decimal 32. )            63 CONSTANT 3F;   ( ;p Hex literal for decimal 63. )                                                                            20; CONSTANT #SP  ( ascii space )                                                                                               : THRU   ( u0 u1 -- )                                              1+ SWAP DO   I LOAD   LOOP   ;                                                                                                                                                    ( cont. )   7 25 THRU                                                      ( -----   Interpreter utilities cont.   )                                                                                       : \   ( input-stream   \ --   ;p comment to end of line )          >IN @ 2-   3F; OR 1+   >IN !   ; IMMEDIATE                                                                                   : SYNONYM   ( defining:   Wordname   \ execution-token -- )                 (    child:   does -- )                                   \ ;p Duplicate the definition of an existing word with          \    another name.                                              \ Usage:   ' 2+ SYNONYM CELL+                                CREATE   ,                                                      DOES>   @ EXECUTE   ;                                                                                                        \ ---                                                                                                                                                                                           \ -----   NOOP ,  Memory word-size definitions                                                                                  : NOOP   ;                                                                                                                      \ ---                                                                                                                           \ -----   Memory word-size definitions   -----                                                                                  ' 2+ SYNONYM CELL+                                                                                                              ' 2* SYNONYM CELLS                                                                                                              ' NOOP SYNONYM BYTES   IMMEDIATE                                                                                                ' + SYNONYM BYTES+   ( addr n -- addr+n )                       \ ---                                                           \ -----   Stack operators                                                                                                       : NIP   ( x0 x1 -- x1 )                                            SWAP DROP   ;                                                                                                                : TUCK   ( x0 x1 -- x1 x0 x1 )                                     SWAP OVER   ;                                                                                                                : PLUCK   ( x0 x1 x2 -- x0 x1 x2 x0 )                              2 PICK   ;                                                                                                                   : -ROT   ( x0 x1 x2 -- x2 x0 x1 )                                  ROT ROT   ;                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         \ cont.  \ -----   Stack operators cont.,   Comparison operators                                                                         : 2DUP   ( x0 x1 -- x0 x1 x0 x1 )                                  OVER OVER   ;                                                                                                                \ ---                                                                                                                                                                                           \ -----   Comparison operators   -----                                                                                          : 0<>   ( w -- Flag )                                              0= 0=   ;                                                                                                                    \ ---                                                                                                                                                                                           \ -----   Math operators,   Logic operators                                                                                     : UMIN   ( u0 u1 -- umin )                                         2DUP U< IF   DROP   ELSE   NIP   THEN   ;                                                                                    \ ---                                                                                                                                                                                           \ -----   Logic operators   -----                                                                                               ' 0<> SYNONYM FLAG   ( flag -- Flag )                              \ ;p Make flag a well-formed Flag.                                                                                           \ ---                                                                                                                                                                                           \ -----   Memory access operators                                                                                               : @+   ( addr -- x addr+2 )                                        DUP @ SWAP CELL+   ;                                                                                                         : !+   ( x addr -- addr+2 )                                        TUCK !   CELL+   ;                                                                                                                                                                           \ Going backwards needs addresses beyond:                       : -C!   ( x addr -- addr-1 )                                       -1 BYTES+   TUCK C!   ;                                                                                                      \ ---                                                                                                                                                                                           \ -----   Compiler syntax utilities                                                                                             : POSTPONE                                                         ( compiling:                Word   \ -- )                       (   non-immediate Word, runtime:   \ -- )   \ COMPILE Word      (      runtime:                         )   \ execute Word      (   immediate Word, runtime:            )   \ execute Word         \ ;p Compile Word such the runtime action is that of            \    Word encountered during compilation.                       \ If Word is immediate, just compile it.  Otherwise,            \ compile COMPILE just before compiling Word.                >IN @   #SP WORD FIND NIP   DUP 0= ABORT" ?"   SWAP >IN !       0< IF   ( non-immediate )   COMPILE COMPILE   THEN              [COMPILE] [COMPILE]   ; IMMEDIATE                                                                                           \ ---                                                            \ -----   Parser utilities                                                                                                      : COMPILING?   ( -- Flag )                                            \ ;p  Is the system currently in the compiling state?        STATE @ FLAG   ;                                                                                                             : <LITERAL>     ( compiling:      x -- )                                        (    runtime:       -- x )                                      ( interpreting:   x -- x )                           \ ;p Process the value of x as if it be a numeral               \    encountered in the syntax.                                 \ Note: non-immediate                                         COMPILING? IF   POSTPONE LITERAL   THEN   ;                                                                                  \ ---                                                                                                                           \ -----   Parser syntax utilities                                                                                               : '>   ( compiling:     Word   \ -- )   ( "tic-of" )                   (    runtime:           \ -- execution-token )                  ( interpreting:  Word   \ -- execution-token )                 \ ;p Tic the next Word in the syntax.                        COMPILING? IF                                                      POSTPONE [']                                                 ELSE                                                               '                                                            THEN   ; IMMEDIATE                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           \ -----   Parser syntax utilities cont.                                                                                         : NUMERAL                                                             ( defining:         Wordname   \ -- )                           (    child, compiling:      does -- )    \ compile value        (       runtime:                 -- x )  \ return value         (    child, interpreting:   does -- x )  \ put on stack         \ ;p  NUMERAL is for values without names.                      \ The child word processes it's value as                        \ if the value be a numeral encountered in the syntax.       CREATE   1 CELLS ALLOT   IMMEDIATE                              DOES>   @   <LITERAL>   ;                                                                                                       \ See VOID and START-VOID for an example of using NUMERAL .  \ ---                                                                                                                           \ -----   Compiler-syntax parser utilities                                                                                      : PARSE>   ( compiling:   Word   \ -- )   ( "parse-word" )                 (    runtime, compiling:   )   \ see POSTPONE Word              (    runtime, interpreting:   )   \ execute Word           \ ;p Compile Word such that the runtime action is that          \    of Word encountered in the syntax.                      POSTPONE   COMPILING?                                              \ Decide at run-time whether to execute or postpone Word.    POSTPONE   IF                                                   >IN @   POSTPONE POSTPONE   >IN !  \ Word is compiled twice     POSTPONE   ELSE                                                 POSTPONE [COMPILE]   \ This needs the execution of Word,                     \ rather than Word's behavior in the compiler.     POSTPONE   THEN   ; IMMEDIATE                                      \ See "> for an example of using PARSE> .       \ cont.   \ -----   PARSE> cont.,   Return stack operators                                                                                \ Implementing PARSE> is awkward, because given a Word's        \ execution token it can't be postponed, and if Word has been   \ postponed it can't be executed.  Therefore, the logic         \ structure is expanded in the definition where PARSE> is used. \ ---                                                                                                                           \ -----   Return stack operators   -----                                                                                        : 2>R   ( x0 x1 --   ;r -- x0 x1 )                                 POSTPONE SWAP   POSTPONE >R   POSTPONE >R   ; IMMEDIATE                                                                      : 2R>   ( -- x0 x1   ;r x0 x1 -- )                                 POSTPONE R>   POSTPONE R>   POSTPONE SWAP   ; IMMEDIATE      \ ---                                                           \ -----   String operators                                                                                                      : MOVE   ( from to len --   ;p data movement )                     PLUCK PLUCK U< IF   CMOVE>   ELSE   CMOVE   THEN   ;                                                                         : MOVE+#SP   ( from to len --   ;p MOVE with appended space )      2DUP 2>R   MOVE   2R> + #SP SWAP C!   ;                                                                                      \ ---                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           \ -----   Interpreter local variables                                                                                           NUMERAL VOID   \ The address of the current data void                                                                           : START-VOID   ( -- )                                                \ p; Initialize a data void.                                  HERE ['] VOID >BODY !   ;                                                                                                    ' START-VOID SYNONYM END-VOID                                      \ ;p A syntax marker to indicate the end of definitions for     \    a void.                                                                                                                 \ ---                                                                                                                           \ -----   End of System Level Definitions ---                                                                                   ( Application Definitions   ******************************** )                                                                                                                                  \ -----   Constants and Structures   -----                                                                                      20; CONSTANT #WORDNAME   \ max length of a wordname, 31 chars,                           \ plus room for one trailing space                                                                     START-VOID                                                                                                                      #WORDNAME 3 CELLS +   ALLOT                                        \ a void for temporarily preserving the first part of TIB       \ and the 3 input-stream variables, BLK , >IN , and #TIB                                                                     \ ---                                                                                                                           \ -----   Structure workers                                                                                                     \ The following three words are used in the common triple of:   \ save-it, set-it, and later, restore-it.                                                                                       : OPEN-TIB   ( -- )   \ save-it                                    BLK @   #TIB @   >IN @                                          VOID !+ !+ !+   TIB SWAP #WORDNAME MOVE   ;                                                                                  : CLOSE-TIB   ( -- )   \ restore-it                                VOID @+ @+ @+   TIB #WORDNAME MOVE                              BLK !   #TIB !   >IN !   ;                                                                                                   END-VOID                                                                                                                                                                               \ cont.  \ -----   Structure workers cont.                                                                                               : PREPARE-TIB   ( addr len -- )   \ set-it                            \ Setup addr len as a Wordname at TIB                        #WORDNAME 1- UMIN DUP >R   TIB SWAP MOVE+#SP                       \ Now, set the input stream to interpreting from TIB                \ This is the key part of this program.                         \ Also, see Appendix B for further details.              R> 1+ #TIB !   0 >IN !   0 BLK !   ;                                                                                         \ ---                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           \ -----   High-level definitions                                : <NAME>   ( addr len <name>Word_execution-token -- )                 \ ;p This word saves TIB and the input stream variables,        \    and moves the user specified string to TIB where it        \    can then be parsed by the user specified                   \    <name>Word.  The input stream is then restored.         -ROT                                                            OPEN-TIB PREPARE-TIB   \ Save-it, set-it,                       EXECUTE                \ execute the <name>Word,                CLOSE-TIB   ;          \ and restore-it.                                                                                     \ With addr len, <NAME> could be used syntactically as:         \    Interpreting:    '  CREATE <NAME>                          \    Compiling:      ['] CREATE <NAME>                          \  or                 '> CREATE <NAME>         ␀                \ ---                                                           \ -----   Syntax definitions                                                                                                    \ A one-word syntax for passing strings to <name>Words.                                                                         : ">   ( <name>Word   \ addr len -- )   ( "quote-to" )                \ <name>Word is a word that accepts a <name>                    \ from the input stream.                                     PARSE> '>   PARSE> <NAME>   ; IMMEDIATE                            \ See Appendix A for usage examples of "> .                                                                               \ ---                                                                                                                           \ -----   End of Application Definitions ---                                                                                    \ -----   End of FORTH-83 Standard Program -----                                                                                ( Appendix A:  Definitions for Testing   ******************* )                                                                  \ -----   An addr-len string is needed for testing   -----                                                                      START-VOID                                                      3 BYTES ALLOT   \ a void for a counted-string dummy wordname                                                                    : STRING0"   ( -- addr len )   ( "string-zero-quote" )                \ ;p returns a string containing  !#                         2   #SP 1+   DUP 2+   VOID 3 BYTES+   -C! -C! -C!               COUNT   ;                                                                                                                    END-VOID                                                          \ Note: This implementation is a way to get a string            \   for testing--there's no  C,  and no  "  handy.            \ ---                                                           \ -----   A routine to test the compiler compgnent of  ">                                                                       \  This routine CREATEs a word named  !#                                                                                        : "CREATE   ( addr len -- )   ( "quote-create" )                   "> CREATE   ;                                                                                                                STRING0" "CREATE                                                                                                                \ ---                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           \ -----   A routine to test the interpreter component of ">                                                                     \  This routine also CREATEs a word named  !#                                                                                   STRING0"   "> CREATE                                                                                                            \ ---                                                                                                                           \ -----   End of Definitions for Testing   ---                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    Appendix B:  FORTH-83 Implementation-Basis Notes  **********                                                                             FORTH-83 Implementation-Basis Notes                                                                                 From _FORTH-83 STANDARD_:                                                                                                       p. 26                                                              #TIB           -- addr                U,83  "number-t-i-b"         ...  #TIB is accessed by WORD when BLK is zero. ...                                                                       p. 31                                                              BLK            -- addr                U,79   "b-l-k"               ...  If the value of BLK is zero the input stream is taken      from the text input buffer.  {{0..the number of blocks          available -1}}  See: TIB  "input stream"                                                                                     -----   Appendix B cont.                                                                                                     p. 6:                                                              input stream                                                       ... Words using or altering BLK , >IN , TIB and #TIB are        responsible for maintaining and restoring control of the        input stream.                                                                                                             p. 38                                                              TIB            -- addr                83     "t-i-b"               The address of the text input buffer...The minimum              capacity of TIB is 80 characters.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            -----   Appendix B cont.                                                                                                     For comparison with the Glossary definitions of the             input-stream user variables:                                                                                                    p. 38                                                              STATE          -- addr                U,79                         ... A Standard Program may not modify this variable.                                                                                                                                      \ -----   End of FORTH-83 Implementation-Basis Notes   ---                                                                                                                                                                                                      \ End QUOTE-TO.SCR _____________________________________________                                                                